1. Where the code came from
---------------------------

2068-Forth was scaffolded on 2026-09-01 (commit 96aea38) from 2068-Leap
(https://github.com/nchiker/2068-Leap, commit 546ceda, V1 kernel), MIT License, same copyright holder.
docs/PROJECT_PLAN.md records which files came from it. At scaffold time six kernel files were byte-identical
to that commit: kernel/bank, kernel/graphics, kernel/interrupt, kernel/io, kernel/math, kernel/storage
(docs/V2/FORTH_V2_REUSE_AUDIT.md, header). The rest of the Forth system (core/, rom/, the demos) was
written for this project.


2. Tools and references
-----------------------

Tools: tools/check_asm.py and tools/check_z80_opcodes.py came from 2068-Leap (docs/PROJECT_PLAN.md);
tools/sjasmplus_strict.sh was first taken from 2068-Leap and is now ported from 2068-Leap-C's
tools/sjasm_strict.sh; tools/z80sim/ is a vendored copy of z80sim commit 13c9bce (tools/z80sim/UPSTREAM.md).
All of these have the same copyright holder and the MIT License. tools/budget.py, tools/provenance.py and
tools/poweron.py follow the design of the scripts of the same names in 2068-Leap-C; the search and
fill logic of provenance.py is ported from it.

Source comments cite, without reproducing them: the "Timex Sinclair 2068 ROM Disassembly" (David Anderson,
2023) and the stock ROM's routine names. The files whose comments mention the disassembly are: core/printer.asm,
core/sound.asm, core/stick.asm, include/hardware.inc, kernel/graphics/graphics.asm,
kernel/interrupt/interrupt.asm, kernel/io/io.asm and kernel/sound/sound.asm (grep, updated in step S:
core/beep.asm no longer mentions it since step A, kernel/storage/storage.asm since step S; kernel/mode64/mode64.asm
was on this list until step M6 removed it, 2026-10-29). A comment that cites the
disassembly does not by itself show that the code was copied or not copied; section 3 is the measurement.

kernel/storage/tapeblock.asm (since step S) is 2068-Leap-C's clean-room tape block I/O
(src/runtime/tapeblock.asm at d31d785; MIT, same copyright holder), its code unchanged; see section 3a and
kernel/storage/UPSTREAM.md, which tools/check_upstream.py enforces in make check.

core/beep.asm (since step A) takes its period table and shift-and-round scheme from 2068-Leap's rom/v2_ay_beep.asm
(MIT, same copyright holder), rebuilt against docs/V2/AY_BEEP_SPEC.md.

The floating point (since step F, docs/V2/FLOAT_SPEC.md): core/fpcore.asm, core/fpparse.asm, core/fpprint.asm and
core/fpconv.asm are rebuilt from 2068-Leap-C's src/runtime/fpcore.asm (its number format, unpacked form and
algorithms; MIT, same copyright holder), with their own multiply and divide kernels and decimal output; the
SQR method and the sine series and its coefficients in core/fpmath.asm come from 2068-Leap's rom/exrom_v2_vm_core.asm
(54cd877) and rom/exrom_v2_float_fn.asm (MIT, same copyright holder). tools/fpmodel.py is 2068-Leap-C's
tools/fpmodel.py, vendored unchanged (its sha256 is recorded in tools/fpforth.py). kernel/math/math.asm's multiply,
divide and random-number routines (since step F, O10) are rebuilt from 2068-Leap-C's src/runtime/helpers.asm and
src/runtime/library.asm (MIT, same copyright holder). Leap-C's fpcore describes itself as the re-engineered
calculator of 2068-Leap's V2, whose calculator had been partly ported from the Spectrum ROM; section 4 records the
owner's position on it.


3. Byte comparison with the stock ROMs (tools/provenance.py, run by make check)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

tools/provenance.py compares the images built from this tree with the stock Spectrum 48K ROM (48.rom) and
the TS2068's HOME ROM and EXROM (tc2068-0.rom, tc2068-1.rom), byte for byte, and lists every common run of
12 bytes or more. Images compared: the Forth HOME ROM and its DOCK-cartridge build, the Blackjack demo
(HOME and cartridge), the Shooting Gallery demo, and the graphics EXROM. Result at the commit that
introduced this file: 45 common runs, in the places a-c below. After step R0 replaced the glyph builder
(b), the result is 25 runs: a and c. After step S replaced the tape block I/O (a), the result is 1 run: c.

  a. Tape block I/O (STORAGE_SEND_BLOCK, STORAGE_RECEIVE_BLOCK): REPLACED in step S. Before, 7 runs of 13
     to 45 bytes (each found in 48.rom and in tc2068-1.rom), in routines whose own header called them a
     port of the stock ROM's SA-BYTES and LD-BYTES; the Forth HOME ROM and its cartridge build only. The
     new code is 2068-Leap-C's src/runtime/tapeblock.asm, adopted with its code unchanged as
     kernel/storage/tapeblock.asm (MIT, same copyright holder). 2068-Leap-C's docs/TAPE_CLEANROOM.md
     records that it was written from a specification of the tape signal by an author who had not seen
     the old code, the stock ROM or a disassembly. That record is 2068-Leap-C's; this repository has not
     re-checked it, and the people who adopted it here had read the old routines, which is why the code
     was adopted unchanged rather than rewritten. What was measured here: the scan finds no 12-byte run
     in it, and the code hashes to Leap-C's (tools/check_upstream.py).

  b. Block-graphics glyph builder (GFX_CHAR_TO_FONT_OFFSET, kernel/graphics/graphics.asm): REPLACED in step
     R0 (audit O2). Before, two 12-byte runs equal to the same bytes of 48.rom and of tc2068-0.rom, in the
     Forth, Blackjack and Gallery images. The new body is the one 2068-Leap-C carries as its delta D21
     (MIT, same copyright holder); 2068-Leap-C's NOTICE and docs/TAPE_CLEANROOM.md record that it was
     written from the screen format alone by an author who had not seen the old code. That record is
     2068-Leap-C's; this repository has not re-checked it. What was measured here: the scan finds no
     12-byte run in the new routine, and rom/test_blockgfx.asm checks that its output matches the glyphs
     the screen format fixes.

  c. One run of 1,171 bytes at the end of graphics_exrom.bin, of which one byte is not the $FF fill; the
     stock ROM also ends in a run of $FF. Not a finding; the script classes it as fill.

Until step S, item a was recorded in tools/provenance.py as allowed debt owned by step S; the list is now
empty, so any new run fails make check.

No run of 12 bytes or more was found anywhere else. In particular none falls in core/printer.asm,
kernel/sound or core/stick.asm, nor did one fall in kernel/mode64 before step M6 removed it. The scan
does not detect code that was rewritten from the disassembly with different bytes, and it does not
examine runs shorter than 12 bytes.

The font (kernel/graphics/font.asm since step F, FONT_TABLE) has no 12-byte run in common with these ROMs. That is all
this file claims about it; the glyph-by-glyph comparison Leap-C's NOTICE makes has not been done here.


4. Owner positions on files this scan covers (decisions of 2026-10-28, audit section 10)
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Both positions are conditional on the scan and are recorded here as conditions, not as conclusions.

- Decision 4 (floating-point core, "fpcore"): the owner accepted, for this NOTICE, the position Leap V2
  takes for its own fpcore (passes the 12-byte scan; MIT), CONDITIONAL on the scan coming back clean on the
  Forth tree. Step F rebuilt fpcore here (section 2). Measured at step F's engine commit (38e318b) and at
  7dd20d8: the scan of all six images reports no run of 12 bytes or more except the fill (c) above, so the
  condition decision 4 states holds at those commits; make check re-runs the scan at every commit.

- Decision 5 (files written while reading the disassembly: core/printer.asm, kernel/sound, kernel/mode64,
  core/stick.asm; step M6 has since removed kernel/mode64): the owner accepted them as they are for now,
  and a clean-room rewrite is to be scheduled only for one of them that the scan flags. The scan flags
  none of them. That is a measurement at one commit, not a permanent clearance; it is re-run by make check whenever the stock ROMs are available.
  The audit's list names four files; section 2 lists others whose comments cite the disassembly (for example
  core/sound.asm and kernel/io/io.asm). Decision 5 does not mention them, and the scan flags
  none of them.
